#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73479c4-84be-5da2-bded-e91dcfd7ee22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{4}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337453} {:answer/id #uuid "971dba84-487b-58a9-b1c1-8e025439e403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{4}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337454} {:answer/id #uuid "3532d1b2-2f7f-5d4f-834f-9b2ab577e04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{3}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337455} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7f4541-a3fd-5656-9251-8a470ff19de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337456} {:answer/id #uuid "c505e7ef-7170-5024-82d9-d10569aca3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{4}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c505e7ef-7170-5024-82d9-d10569aca3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{4}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337457} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bcee313-9598-5855-8424-1acba228c53f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64a47477-7e97-505b-9a22-72d4f75d41db" :question/math-academy-id "q-122389" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+400{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+400{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=400{x}^{6}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 20{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 20\\int {x}^{3}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm 5{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-5{x}^{4}+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e50a56fa-b0ec-5062-8484-87dd0858b0f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}+{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337460} {:answer/id #uuid "f4efee2d-c776-5837-86fe-fb3bed6fba84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{8}+2{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337461} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4d115a-7ff6-5f6c-86e4-11281d750c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{12}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337462} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e4751-073e-50f4-9974-bad132c2770c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4{x}^{6}+2{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337463} {:answer/id #uuid "e71f96ed-ccf8-5505-b53b-25f839de1752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e71f96ed-ccf8-5505-b53b-25f839de1752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337464} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e0eb18-8712-55ca-9ee8-15ec6d67ba55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891e62c5-2f59-5791-9c12-b3816ce7c5d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15403" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2}$ on $[0,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{3}{2})6{x}^{2}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{1/2}={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}(2{x}^{3}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e29027-cd8f-5288-ad2d-035977d9cb75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}+\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82e29027-cd8f-5288-ad2d-035977d9cb75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}+\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298887} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0d4249a-3912-5dc4-871a-c97b49be5238" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e883a7d-82a7-5e23-83d2-65862f5b051d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353183" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in [1,2].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+16}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f82886a-1596-597b-aa99-eb7231f24c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081801} {:answer/id #uuid "d4250250-9bb4-55e0-84ce-cc141031bb47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081802} {:answer/id #uuid "19486551-4321-5db4-bf17-5afce84bb446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081803} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4c9c2e-60c1-5919-85e7-dc75faff1be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081804} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff9df43-d8c6-50e2-8ff3-b8b5320b1178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f82886a-1596-597b-aa99-eb7231f24c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081801} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d8d5f50-6677-55fd-b765-e50cba8492fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "455744bf-a0d3-559d-ac0d-1efc9eef0e22" :question/math-academy-id "q-5983" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2}{3}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3/2},$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=2x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[2x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=2{x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(4)^{3}+(4)]-[\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)] \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18433ded-ba7e-52ee-8921-d06888eea8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337467} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6b9962-a78d-520c-a5f1-4696af0045af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337468} {:answer/id #uuid "d80f015c-9f57-57ff-b3b3-76968703a7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337469} {:answer/id #uuid "e373f9c7-6b4f-56c1-aa9c-8d907c144ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1-x}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337470} {:answer/id #uuid "5459e367-7b1c-5808-9d42-ba9227ea2a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5459e367-7b1c-5808-9d42-ba9227ea2a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337471} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9f9ce59-0774-5034-a0a7-9893cebb905f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d968cc2-d41a-56be-9db0-c9b59a5e633c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82619" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|)+C \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7.$"}]}
